{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 13,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.069,
            0.145,
            0.085
          ],
          "content": "54"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.184,
            0.069,
            0.489,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Manjari | Grade 9 | Part I"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.108,
            0.886,
            0.167
          ],
          "content": "technique called **Proof by Contradiction**. Namely, we assume the opposite of what we want to prove, and show that this assumption leads to a logical disaster."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.171,
            0.611,
            0.193
          ],
          "content": "**Proof:** We shall explain the proof in steps."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.199,
            0.887,
            0.303
          ],
          "content": "**Step 1: Assumption:** Assume $\\sqrt { 2 }$ is a rational number. Therefore, it can be written as a fraction $\\frac { p } { q } , q \\neq 0$ in its simplest form. (This means p and $q$ are integers that share no common factors other than 1, that is, they are co-prime.)"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.488,
            0.305,
            0.568,
            0.346
          ],
          "content": "{ \\sqrt { 2 } } = { \\frac { p } { q } }",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.351,
            0.605,
            0.373
          ],
          "content": "**Step 2:** Square both sides of the equation:"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.505,
            0.377,
            0.577,
            0.426
          ],
          "content": "2   =   \\frac { p ^ { 2 } } { q _ { \\circ } ^ { 2 } }",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.426,
            0.509,
            0.449
          ],
          "content": "**Step 3:** Multiply both sides by $q ^ { 2 } 1$ :"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.483,
            0.451,
            0.573,
            0.474
          ],
          "content": "2q^{2} \\equiv p^{2}",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.477,
            0.421,
            0.498
          ],
          "content": "**Step 4: Deduction** for $p ;$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.504,
            0.885,
            0.566
          ],
          "content": "Because $p ^ { 2 }$ is equal to 2 times some integer, $p ^ { 2 }$ must be an even number. If the square of a number is even, the number itself must be even. Therefore, p is an even integer. Let us say $\\bar { p } \\bar { \\mathit { \\Gamma } } _ { \\bar { \\mathit { \\Gamma } } } ^ { \\mathit { \\Gamma } } 2 k$ (where k is an integer)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.578,
            0.804,
            0.6
          ],
          "content": "**Step 5:** Substitute $\\bar { p } _ { \\parallel } = 2 k$ back into our equation from Step 3:"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.446,
            0.603,
            0.565,
            0.65
          ],
          "content": "\\frac{2\\bar{q}^{2}=(2k)^{2}}{2q^{2}=4k^{2}}",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.664,
            0.482,
            0.685
          ],
          "content": "**Step 6:** Divide both sides by 2:"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.463,
            0.687,
            0.55,
            0.709
          ],
          "content": "q ^ { 2 } = 2 k ^ { 2 }",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_15.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.722,
            0.886,
            0.784
          ],
          "content": "**Step 7: Deduction** for $q ;$ Now we see that $q ^ { 2 }$ is equal to 2 times some integer $k ^ { 2 } .$ This means $q ^ { 2 }$ is even, and therefore, $q$ must also be an even integer."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.796,
            0.886,
            0.872
          ],
          "content": "**Step 8: The Contradiction:** We deduced that $p$ is even and $q$ is even. This means they both share a common factor of 2. However, in Step 1, we stated that $\\frac { p } { q }$ was in simplest form, sharing no common factors!"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
